FATAL_OBJECTIONS:
  - "STRAWMAN BASELINE: The claim premises 'document-level near-dedup is the standard contamination practice' — it is NOT. GPT-3 (Brown et al. 2020) uses 13-gram overlap; PaLM (Chowdhery et al. 2022) uses 8-gram ≥70% containment. The standard contamination-detection practice IS ALREADY passage/n-gram-level. Document-level Jaccard near-dedup is for corpus deduplication, a different task with a different goal. The headline '0% reported vs 20% true' compares a corpus-dedup tool against a contamination-detection tool and discovers the latter detects contamination better — this is tool-purpose mismatch, not a novel gap. Raised independently by novelty_killer (FATAL), evaluation_prosecutor (STRAWMAN_RISKS), and theory_skeptic (CONFOUNDS). Unrebutted."
  - "ALGEBRAIC TAUTOLOGY: The ~100% miss gap at span_ratio≤0.1 is predicted in closed form: Jaccard(shingles(doc), shingles(item)) ≈ s/(s+D) = span_ratio for disjoint vocab. For 50-in-500, Jaccard≈0.09 < any near-dup threshold (≥0.3). The paper itself derives this arithmetic (RESULTS.md: 'union normalization … 43/490 ≈ 0.09') and measures exactly the predicted value. A measurement that confirms textbook set-arithmetic to 3 decimal places is calibration, not discovery. Raised by theory_skeptic (MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS) and evaluation_prosecutor (STRAWMAN_RISKS). Unrebutted."
  - "MISSING OBVIOUS COMPETITOR METRIC: Containment = |A∩B|/|B| (intersection over item length) is a well-known document-level metric that would yield Containment(item, doc) = 1.0 for any fully verbatim-embedded item regardless of doc length — trivially closing the gap. Its omission from the sweep inflates the apparent novelty of 'document-level granularity is fundamentally blind.' It shows only that ONE doc-level metric (union-normalized Jaccard) is blind, which is again predicted analytically. Raised by evaluation_prosecutor (KILLER_EXPERIMENT) and theory_skeptic (ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED). Unrebutted."
  - "FULL PRIOR-ART OCCUPATION: Lee et al. 2021 (arXiv:2107.06499) introduced NearDup vs ExactSubstr PRECISELY because doc-level near-dedup misses short shared substrings — the mechanism IS the stated motivation for substring-level dedup. The paraphrase fragility is published by Yang et al. 2023 (arXiv:2311.04850). The 2024 contamination surveys (Xu arXiv:2406.04244; Ravaut arXiv:2404.00699) taxonomize string/n-gram vs embedding detection and document paraphrase fragility. Every axis of the claimed contribution (mechanism, rate-distortion, paraphrase boundary) is occupied. Verified across ≥2 independent sources by novelty_killer. Unrebutted."
